54026	{"artifactId":"DEF-2ff0dfdadb1c8d1c","entry":{"id":"migrated-DEF-2ff0dfdadb1c8d1c-review","actor":{"id":"insrc:daemon","kind":"agent","properties":{}},"timestamp":"2026-10-07T07:16:02.128Z","operation":"review","outcome":{"result":"block","summary":"review: block","data":{"kind":"review","report":{"artifact":"define","stage":"define","verdict":"block","findings":[{"claimId":"p1","ref":"c3","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/chain.ts:30-65 defines ChainReport whose stories carry id, title, hasLld, approved, stale fields and amendments — covering only DEF/HLD/LLD, with no plan, build, code-review or issue state.","evidence":"The citation resolves at both ends. `read src/workflow/chain.ts:30` returns `export interface ChainReport {` and `read src/workflow/chain.ts:65` returns `}`, so 30-65 is the interface body. `grep /readonly amendments/` matches `src/workflow/chain.ts:54: readonly amendments: {`, which is inside that span. It also matches `chain.ts:225: readonly amendments: ChainReport['amendments'];`, which uses the type somewhere else. `grep /interface ChainReport/` finds only one definition, at `src/workflow/chain.ts:30`. The three other matches are in docs. Two of those are S003 LLD.md:224 and PLAN.md:110, and both independently confirm the same anchor: \"src/workflow/chain.ts:30 'export interface ChainReport {' resolves exactly\". S003 LLD.md:207 describes the shape as `{ epicHash, epicSlug?, define, hld, stories[], amendments, tracker, nextAction }`. That fits a report that covers only DEF, HLD and LLD, with no plan, build, code-review or issue fields. The per-story fields `id/title/hasLld/approved/stale` show up in the DEF's own quote (DEF.md:202) and in the plugin's StoryChainMark, which mirrors them (WorkflowChainReader.kt:32). The `hasLld` grep was cut off at its cap before it reached a `chain.ts` line, so the story field list was never read directly from the source. The claim that there is no plan, build, code-review or issue state is only supported indirectly, through the LLD.md:207 field summary. None of this changes what gets built, because the anchor, the entity and the scope all hold.","action":"None — verified sound. If you want, read src/workflow/chain.ts:30-65 directly to confirm the story field list and that there is no plan, build, code-review or issue state. The grep evidence was truncated before it reached those lines.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both ends of the cited span (chain.ts:30 and :65) resolve to the ChainReport interface, and the amendments field (:54) falls inside it. The field summary in the S003 artifacts agrees that the report covers only DEF, HLD and LLD. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p2","ref":"c3","kind":"closed-union","severity":"MED","premise":"The existing chain report covers only an epic's framing (DEF), HLD and per-story LLDs; ChainReport has no PLAN, BUILD, CR or ISSUE fields.","evidence":"The only evidence about the anchor is `read src/workflow/chain.ts:30 → FOUND: export interface ChainReport {`. That shows the interface exists at the cited place, but the read stops at the opening line, so none of ChainReport's fields are visible. The grep /PLAN|BUILD|ISSUE|'CR'/ returned 50 matches and was TRUNCATED at the cap. Every match shown is in docs/ (docs/artifact-document-template-review.md and the bugfix epic's HLD.md, S002/LLD.md and S002/PLAN.md); none is in src/workflow/chain.ts. Because the result was truncated, the missing chain.ts matches do not prove that ChainReport lacks PLAN, BUILD, CR or ISSUE fields. Some docs material is related but not conclusive. docs/artifact-document-template-review.md:43 describes the chain on disk as \"DEF → HLD → `S00n/{LLD,PLAN,BUILD,CR}`\", which is the folder tree and not the ChainReport type. S002/LLD.md:154-155 shows that ArtifactKind already includes PLAN, BUILD and CR and gains ISSUE. That means those kinds exist elsewhere in the framework, but it says nothing about ChainReport's fields. The premise's central claim, that the type is a closed set covering only DEF, HLD and per-story LLDs, is therefore neither confirmed nor contradicted by the evidence.","action":"Re-run the probe with full coverage of ChainReport: read src/workflow/chain.ts from line 30 to the closing brace of `export interface ChainReport`, and grep only src/workflow/chain.ts for /plan|build|codeReview|cr\\b|issue/i so the result is not truncated by docs matches. If the type has only define, hld and per-story lld fields, mark the premise verified. If it already has any of PLAN, BUILD, CR or ISSUE, correct the premise and reduce the task's scope to the kinds that are actually missing.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor resolves to ChainReport, so the concept is right, but the evidence does not show its fields. No edit can be derived from it until the full interface body has been read.","options":["Re-probe: read the full ChainReport body at src/workflow/chain.ts:30 and grep only chain.ts, then confirm the premise as written.","Keep the premise but mark it as an unverified assumption the build task must check first: read ChainReport and add only the PLAN/BUILD/CR/ISSUE fields that are missing.","Reword the premise to the part the evidence supports (ChainReport is declared at src/workflow/chain.ts:30) and move the field inventory into the task as a discovery step."]}},{"claimId":"p3","ref":"c4","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/pending.ts:126-128 treats pending as neither meta.approvedAt nor meta.rejectedAt stamped, with comment 'Pending = neither stamped: the SAME fields approve/reject write (k5)'.","evidence":"The read of src/workflow/pending.ts:126 returns the cited comment word for word: \"// Pending = neither stamped: the SAME fields approve/reject write (k5).\" The grep for /Pending = neither stamped/ finds only this source line, plus the DEF.md:203 citation that quotes it. The read of src/workflow/pending.ts:128 returns `if (typeof meta['rejectedAt'] === 'string' && meta['rejectedAt'].length > 0) continue;`, so an artifact with a rejectedAt stamp is not counted as pending. Line 127 was not read directly, and the approvedAt grep stopped at its 50-match cap before reaching it. Even so, the comment at :126 says \"neither stamped\", the cited range is :126-128, and :128 is the rejectedAt half of the check, so :127 is almost certainly the matching approvedAt skip. The same reading appears independently in docs/insrc-delivery-board-prd.html:175 (\"Pending means neither stamp is present\") and in docs/plans/delivery-board-epics.md:34, which cites `rejectedAt` (`:126-128`). The evidence supports both the anchor and the premise.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited comment at pending.ts:126 and the rejectedAt check at :128 match the premise exactly. Line 127 was not read directly but is the approvedAt half implied by the comment and the cited range. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p4","ref":"c4","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The approve path stamps meta.approvedAt at src/workflow/gates.ts:606 and the reject path stamps meta.rejectedAt at src/workflow/gates.ts:954.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve to the right code. `read src/workflow/gates.ts:606` returns `const approvedAt = new Date().toISOString();`. `read src/workflow/gates.ts:954` returns `const rejectedAt = new Date().toISOString();`. The rejectedAt grep also matches `src/workflow/gates.ts:937: readonly rejectedAt: string;`, a field on a result type just above line 954, so line 954 is inside the reject path. One detail is slightly off: both lines create the timestamp in a local variable. The `meta.approvedAt` / `meta.rejectedAt` field is presumably written a few lines further down, which the evidence does not show. The anchors still point at the right concept and the right code paths, so building against them would produce correct code.","action":"None needed; the citation checks out. Optionally, reword it to say that these lines create the approval/rejection timestamps, which are then stored in meta.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both anchors resolve to the timestamp assignment in the approve path and the reject path. The only imprecision is calling the local-variable line the place where meta is stamped, and that changes nothing about what gets built."}},{"claimId":"p5","ref":"c5","kind":"inventory","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/pending.ts:39 declares PENDING_KINDS = ['SPEC','DEF','HLD','LLD','PLAN','ISSUE','CR'] — seven kinds, excluding BUILD, so the pending list cannot report completion.","evidence":"read src/workflow/pending.ts:39 → FOUND: \"export const PENDING_KINDS = ['SPEC', 'DEF', 'HLD', 'LLD', 'PLAN', 'ISSUE', 'CR'] as const;\". That is seven members (SPEC, DEF, HLD, LLD, PLAN, ISSUE, CR), and BUILD is not one of them. The anchor line matches the premise exactly. Line 40 derives `PendingKind` from this tuple, and line 87 builds `PENDING_KIND_SET` from it. Because the pending kind set comes only from this list, BUILD artifacts cannot show up as pending, which supports the claim that the pending list cannot report completion. docs/plans/delivery-board-epics.md:34 says the same thing independently: \"`PENDING_KINDS` (`:39`) excludes BUILD\".","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise matches the source: the anchor, the member list and the count of seven are all correct, and BUILD is excluded. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p6","ref":"c6","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/id.ts exports storyIdToOrdinal, under which 's1', 'S1' and 'S001' all yield ordinal 1.","evidence":"The grep finds the export at `src/workflow/id.ts:119` (`export function storyIdToOrdinal(storyId: string): number {`). The function's own comment at `src/workflow/id.ts:122` says: \"The ordinal is what matters — 's1', 'S1' and 'S001' all yield 1.\" A test at `src/workflow/__tests__/id-resolve.test.ts:141` says the same: \"'s1', 'S1' and 'S001' all denote ordinal 1.\" This matches the earlier S001 PLAN step that widened the regex to /^[sS](\\d+)$/. The `path:line` read of src/workflow/id.ts came back NOT FOUND, so the regex body was not shown directly. Still, the export, the comment inside the function and the test assertion all agree with the premise, and none of the evidence contradicts it.","action":"None: the premise is verified sound.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The export exists at id.ts:119. Its own comment at id.ts:122 and the test at id-resolve.test.ts:141 both state the case- and zero-padding-insensitive ordinal behavior. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p7","ref":"c7","kind":"cross-artifact","severity":"LOW","premise":"The prior HLD at docs/epics/restructure-docs-artifact-markdown-from-flat-E20260915599a9b50/HLD.md defines sc1 'Uniform work-item identity' as the canonical both-way identity for every work item and story.","evidence":"The grep for /Uniform work-item identity/ finds the heading `### sc1: Uniform work-item identity` at docs/epics/restructure-docs-artifact-markdown-from-flat-E20260915599a9b50/HLD.md:15. That confirms the cited file exists and that sc1 has this title. Three sibling LLDs in the same epic refer to it consistently: S001/LLD.md:13 says \"**Owns:** `sc1` (Uniform work-item identity)\", and S002/LLD.md:14 and S003/LLD.md:13 say \"**Consumes:** `sc1`\". The citation in this DEF (DEF.md:206) quotes sc1 as \"One canonical, both-way identity for every work item and story — epic-parented or standalone…\", which matches the premise. One gap: the direct read of HLD.md returned NOT FOUND, even though grep matched line 15 of that same file. So the sc1 body text was not re-read here. The \"canonical both-way\" wording rests on the DEF's quote, and the HLD's own heading and its owner/consumer LLDs back it up. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise.","action":"none — verified sound. Optional: re-read HLD.md near line 15 to check the sc1 body word for word, since the direct read returned NOT FOUND even though grep matched that file. The NOT FOUND looks like a problem with the probe's path handling, not with the artifact.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise holds: grep confirms that sc1 'Uniform work-item identity' exists at HLD.md:15, and the sibling LLDs consistently treat it as the identity contract. The artifact needs no edit."}},{"claimId":"p8","ref":"c8","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md:135 contains key architectural rule 1: 'Daemon owns all DB access — CLI, MCP, and the IDE workbench communicate via IPC only.'","evidence":"read CLAUDE.md:135 → FOUND: \"1. **Daemon owns all DB access** — CLI, MCP, and the IDE workbench communicate via IPC only.\" The grep /Daemon owns all DB access/ also matches CLAUDE.md:135 with the same text (AGENTS.md:135 has an identical mirror line). The cited anchor resolves to rule 1 of the key architectural rules, and its wording matches the premise exactly.","action":"None. The citation checks out.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Checked against the source: the citation's anchor and quoted text both match CLAUDE.md:135, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p9","ref":"c9","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md:30-32 states IPC method names, socket path and payload shapes stay in lock-step across the two repos via mirrored types.","evidence":"The engine read CLAUDE.md:30 and found \"clones + builds this repo into `~/.insrc/daemon/`. IPC method names,\". It read CLAUDE.md:32 and found \"lock-step across the two repos via mirrored types.\" Line 31 was not read on its own. Lines 30 and 32 begin and end the cited sentence, and the project's CLAUDE.md as loaded in context gives the full sentence: \"IPC method names, socket path (`~/.insrc/daemon.sock`), and payload shapes stay in lock-step across the two repos via mirrored types.\" The only grep match for /stay in lock-step across the two repos/ is the DEF.md:208 citation entry itself, which quotes the same text. The line range 30-32 and the claim's content both match the source.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"No fix needed. CLAUDE.md:30-32 says what citation c9 claims. The DEF labels it with the section 'What lives here'; in the file, the sentence sits in the closing paragraph about what the IDE fork owns, which is still inside that section. The label is accurate."}},{"claimId":"p10","ref":"c10","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"CLAUDE.md:11 states 'Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority.'","evidence":"The read of CLAUDE.md:11 returned FOUND: \"- **Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority.** When choosing between an accurate-but-expensive path ... choose accuracy.\" The grep for /Accuracy is primary; cost is the least priority/ also matches CLAUDE.md:11, so the cited line holds exactly the quoted principle. Other files also match, such as AGENTS.md:11 and README.md:72, but the anchor is correct.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The citation resolves to the right line and the quoted text matches exactly. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p11","ref":"c11","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/daemon/index.ts registers workflow.approve at :643, workflow.pending at :686, workflow.artifactContent at :698, workflow.resolveComment at :712, artifact.get at :1131 and artifact.search at :1145.","evidence":"All six anchors check out against the source. Each line opens the handler the premise names: src/daemon/index.ts:643 `'workflow.approve': async (params) => {`, :686 `'workflow.pending': async (params) => {`, :698 `'workflow.artifactContent': async (params) => {`, :712 `'workflow.resolveComment': async (params) => {`, :1131 `'artifact.get': async (params) => {`, :1145 `'artifact.search': async (params) => {`. A grep for each method name finds exactly one registration under src/daemon, at the cited line. The only other hits are client callers (src/mcp/daemon-stream.ts:289 and vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-client.ts) and older docs. Those docs cite stale line numbers such as :551, :555, :579, :634, :980 and :994, but they don't affect this premise.","action":"None. The citation is verified sound.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Each cited file:line opens the handler it names, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p12","ref":"c12","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/types.ts:401 declares meta.sizeClass?: SizeClass and :412 declares meta.magnitude?: BugfixMagnitude.","evidence":"The read of src/workflow/types.ts:401 returns `readonly sizeClass?:   SizeClass;` and the read of src/workflow/types.ts:412 returns `readonly magnitude?:   BugfixMagnitude;`. Both greps also match at those exact lines (`src/workflow/types.ts:401` for /sizeClass\\?:\\s*SizeClass/ and `src/workflow/types.ts:412` for /magnitude\\?:\\s*BugfixMagnitude/). Both anchors point to the declarations the premise names.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both cited lines point to the declared fields exactly, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p13","ref":"c12","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"SizeClass at src/workflow/triage/types.ts:17 is exactly the five-member union 'epic' | 'feature' | 'small' | 'trivial' | 'bugfix'.","evidence":"The read of src/workflow/triage/types.ts:17 returned `export type SizeClass = 'epic' | 'feature' | 'small' | 'trivial' | 'bugfix';`. That is exactly the five-member union the premise claims, at the cited line. The grep for /type SizeClass\\s*=/ found only one definition in source (src/workflow/triage/types.ts:17). The other four matches are in design docs: the bugfix epic's HLD.md:26, its S001 LLD.md:74-75 diff, and an older S009 LLD review row that quotes the four-member type from before 'bugfix' was added at line 16. None of them is a competing source definition.","action":"None needed. The premise is confirmed by the source.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Confirmed by the source: the anchor resolves to the claimed five-member union, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p14","ref":"c12","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/orchestrator.ts:2352 stamps sizeClass on a standalone design.story LLD with only a defined-check (sizeClass !== undefined), and :903 stamps magnitude on an ISSUE only when it is 'small' or 'sized'.","evidence":"read src/workflow/orchestrator.ts:2352 → `...(sizeClass !== undefined ? { sizeClass } : {}),`, which is a defined-check only. read src/workflow/orchestrator.ts:903 → `...(magnitude === 'small' || magnitude === 'sized' ? { magnitude } : {}),`, so magnitude is stamped only when it is 'small' or 'sized'. Both grep probes match these exact lines in the source. The only other match is the DEF.md citation itself. The evidence covers the stamping lines only. It does not show whether :2352 is in the standalone design.story path or whether :903 is in the ISSUE path, so those parts of the premise are not checked here.","action":"None. The premise holds: both cited lines resolve to the exact guards described.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited file:line anchors and their guard expressions match the source exactly, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p15","ref":"c12","kind":"closed-union","severity":"LOW","premise":"BugfixMagnitude is restricted to 'small' | 'sized'.","evidence":"The source defines the type at src/workflow/triage/types.ts:24 as `export type BugfixMagnitude = 'small' | 'sized';`. That is a closed union of exactly two string literals, which matches the premise. The design docs agree with each other: HLD.md:29, S001/LLD.md:98, :101 and :245, and S001/PLAN.md:30 all declare the same `'small' | 'sized'` union, and none of the six matches adds a third member.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The premise matches the real type at src/workflow/triage/types.ts:24, so no edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p16","ref":"c18","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/storage.ts:77-87 defines writeAtomic which writes a temp file and renameSync's it into place.","evidence":"Both anchors resolve as the premise says. Reading src/workflow/storage.ts:77 shows `export function writeAtomic(absPath: string, content: string): void {`, and grep finds exactly one definition at that line outside the DEF itself. Reading src/workflow/storage.ts:87 shows `renameSync(tmp, absPath);`, which closes the cited 77-87 range. A rename from a `tmp` path into `absPath` is the write-to-temp-then-rename pattern the premise describes. The temp-file write between those lines was not read directly, but `renameSync(tmp, absPath)` only makes sense if a temp file was written first. The other `renameSync(tmp` matches (analyze/*/cache.ts, persistence.ts, config/reconcile-io.ts) are separate helpers and do not compete with this anchor.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The citation resolves to the correct entity: writeAtomic is at line 77 and renameSync(tmp, absPath) is at line 87. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p17","ref":"c18","kind":"semantic","severity":"LOW","premise":"An artifact's JSON record and its markdown copy are written by two separate writeAtomic calls, not committed together.","evidence":"The evidence supports the premise. `src/workflow/storage.ts:77` defines `export function writeAtomic(absPath: string, content: string): void`, which writes one path per call (the DEF citation shows it ends in `renameSync(tmp, absPath)`). Every artifact-persist call site writes the markdown and the JSON with two separate, back-to-back calls and nothing tying them together: `src/daemon/workflow-rpc.ts:334-335` (`writeAtomic(paths.md, finalized.renderedMd); writeAtomic(paths.json, finalized.renderedJson);`), `src/mcp/workflow-step/phases/synthesize.ts:82-83` (same pair), `src/mcp/review-step/phases/findings.ts:91-92` (jsonPath, then mdPath), and `src/mcp/review-step/phases/verdicts.ts:96,98` (jsonPath, then mdPath). The test fixtures follow the same pattern, for example `plan-artifact.test.ts:217-218` and `seed-focus.test.ts:62-63`. None of the matches shows a multi-file transaction or a combined commit.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The evidence confirms the premise: each writeAtomic call atomically renames a single file, and the artifact's md and json are always written with two separate calls (workflow-rpc.ts:334-335, synthesize.ts:82-83, findings.ts:91-92, verdicts.ts:96/98). No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p18","ref":"c19","kind":"external-contract","severity":"MED","premise":"The daemon socket server (src/daemon/server.ts:59-60, 164-242) only answers requests with a single response or a request-scoped stream (stream:done / stream:error); it never sends unsolicited messages, so there is no subscription/push channel.","evidence":"The mechanism half of the premise is confirmed. src/daemon/server.ts:59 reads \"Stream mode: request with `\"stream\": true` gets multiple IpcStreamMessages\", and :60 reads \"until the handler resolves (stream:done) or throws (stream:error)\". Line 164 is \"// Check for streaming request\" and line 242 is `socket.write(JSON.stringify(response) + '\\n');`, the single-response path. The absence half (\"never sends unsolicited messages\") is not established. The grep /subscribe|notify|broadcast/ was TRUNCATED at 50 matches. None of the matches shown are in src/daemon/, so they neither confirm nor rule out a push path in server.ts or the handlers. The evidence also shows request-scoped streams already used as subscriptions. The JetBrains DEF d7 notes LogsSection.tsx \"subscribes via debug.tailLog (:85)\". The artifact-review HLD:219 proposes \"a long-lived streaming subscribe handler (the socket server already supports per-request streaming)\". So the claim \"there is no subscription/push channel\" is too strong. There is no unsolicited broadcast, but a long-lived stream request is an established way to subscribe.","action":"Reword c19 to keep the verified fact: every server write answers a request, either as one response or as a request-scoped stream that ends with stream:done or stream:error. Narrow the conclusion to: there is no unsolicited, connection-level broadcast, and push-style delivery is only possible as a long-lived streamed request (the debug.tailLog pattern). Also confirm absence by grepping src/daemon/ (not the whole repo) for any other socket.write, broadcast or emit path outside the request handler.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The stream:done / stream:error mechanism is confirmed at server.ts:59-60. The 'no subscription channel' conclusion is overstated: debug.tailLog already uses a long-lived request-scoped stream as a subscription, and the absence check came from a truncated repo-wide grep. Which wording is right depends on the design decision this premise supports, so it needs a human choice rather than an automatic edit.","options":["Narrow the premise: 'the server never sends unsolicited, connection-level messages; push delivery is only possible as a long-lived request-scoped stream (as debug.tailLog does), ending with stream:done or stream:error'.","Keep the premise but add the debug.tailLog stream as the existing subscribe-by-stream precedent, and state that real-time push is a non-goal rather than impossible.","Re-verify absence with a grep scoped to src/daemon/ for socket.write, emit and broadcast outside the request handler before keeping the 'never sends unsolicited messages' wording."]}},{"claimId":"p19","ref":"c16","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"The terminal tool reads the artifact store directly: src/cli/services/workflow.ts:126 listEpics joins repoPath with ARTIFACTS_DIR and :145 chain calls buildChainReport(repoPath, epicHash) in-process.","evidence":"read src/cli/services/workflow.ts:126 → `const dir = join(repoPath, ARTIFACTS_DIR);` (the listEpics directory join, also matched by grep /join\\(repoPath, ARTIFACTS_DIR\\)/). read src/cli/services/workflow.ts:145 → `return buildChainReport(repoPath, epicHash);` (the chain call, also matched by grep; a second in-process use sits at :150 `formatChainReport(buildChainReport(repoPath, epicHash))`). Both anchors resolve to exactly the cited code, so the premise holds: the terminal tool's CLI service reads the artifact store directly and builds the chain report in the same process.","action":"none — verified sound","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both cited file:line anchors resolve to the quoted code. No correction needed. (Optional: :150 is a second in-process buildChainReport call site, which supports the premise but isn't required.)"}},{"claimId":"p20","ref":"c20","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-client.ts:18 imports type PendingArtifact from the daemon's src/workflow/pending.js, and jetbrains-plugin DaemonGateway.kt:31 declares data class PendingArtifactDto.","evidence":"Both anchors match. Reading vscode-plugin/src/chat/docs-review-client.ts:18 returns `import type { PendingArtifact } from '../../../src/workflow/pending.js';`, which is a type-only import of PendingArtifact from the daemon's src/workflow/pending.js. Reading jetbrains-plugin/src/main/kotlin/ai/insors/insrc/jetbrains/daemon/DaemonGateway.kt:31 returns `data class PendingArtifactDto(`. Grep found one more copy of the Kotlin class at jetbrains-plugin/bin/main/.../DaemonGateway.kt:31. That copy is a build-output mirror, not a competing source, and the cited src/main path is the right one.","action":"None needed. The citation checks out against the source.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"Both cited file:line anchors point at the exact text the premise describes. No change is needed."}},{"claimId":"p21","ref":"c21","kind":"citation","severity":"LOW","premise":"src/workflow/artifact-content.ts:101 defines handleArtifactContent; :119-125 restricts to .md under docs/; :156-162 resolves metadata via the in-file insrc:artifact marker through jsonPathForMd and returns an error if unresolvable.","evidence":"The engine's reads match each anchor. src/workflow/artifact-content.ts:101 is `export function handleArtifactContent(` (the grep agrees). Line 119 is `const rel = relative(docsRoot, absMd);`, which is the start of a check that the path stays under docs/. Line 156 is the comment `// The sibling hash-flat .json (meta + body) via the in-file insrc:artifact marker.`. Line 161 returns `{ error: \\`workflow.artifactContent: cannot resolve artifact metadata: ${(err as Error).message}\\` }`, which is inside the cited :156-162 range. Two parts are not directly shown. First, the `.md` extension check within :119-125, since only line 119 was read. Second, the jsonPathForMd call in artifact-content.ts, because the jsonPathForMd grep stopped at its 50-match cap before it listed any src/ file. Both are consistent with the code that was read: there is a docs-root containment check, and the comment names the marker-based resolution. Nothing in the evidence contradicts the premise.","action":"None needed; the premise checks out. Optionally, re-read src/workflow/artifact-content.ts:119-125 and :156-160 to confirm the `.md` extension check and the jsonPathForMd call directly.","fixability":"manual","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The cited anchors resolve to the claimed entities: the handler at :101, the docs-root containment check at :119, the marker-resolution comment at :156, and the metadata-resolution error at :161. No edit is needed."}},{"claimId":"p22","ref":"c21","kind":"inventory","severity":"MED","premise":"69 BUILD records have markdown lacking the insrc:artifact marker and thus cannot be opened through workflow.artifactContent.","evidence":"The only probe was `grep /insrc:artifact/`, which returned 50 matches and was TRUNCATED at the cap. Every match is either a line-1 marker in a DEF/HLD/LLD/PLAN/CR markdown under docs/epics/ (for example `docs/epics/add-bugfix-triage-category-insrc-framework-E202609181703991c/S001/CR.md:1:<!-- insrc:artifact CR-1703991c69967193-s1 -->`) or a prose mention in docs. Examples of the prose mentions are docs/artifact-document-template-review.md:30 and :103, and docs/epics/add-build-workflow-insrc-5th-stage-E20260717185807ba/S005/LLD.md:87, :178 and :201. None of the matches is a BUILD record, and nothing comes from `.insrc/artifacts`. No probe listed BUILD record markdowns, so there is no total to compare against 69. No read of `workflow.artifactContent` was gathered either, so the claimed mechanism (no marker means the file cannot be opened) is also unshown. The evidence does not address the premise in either direction, so it is UNVERIFIABLE.","action":"Re-ground the premise with probes that answer it directly. (1) List every BUILD record markdown under `.insrc/artifacts` and wherever else BUILD md files are written, and count the ones with no `<!-- insrc:artifact BUILD-` line in their first 4096 characters. That is the head-slice limit cited at gates.ts:807 and migrate-docs-tree.ts:112. (2) Read how `workflow.artifactContent` resolves an md to its record, and confirm it fails when the marker is missing rather than falling back to hash-named json. Then restate the count of 69 from the measured number, or remove it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The gathered evidence neither confirms nor contradicts the count of 69 or the artifactContent failure mode, so no edit can be derived from it. The premise needs re-measuring before the number is kept.","options":["Re-run the probe scoped to BUILD md files (e.g. find BUILD*.md under .insrc/artifacts and docs/epics, then grep -L 'insrc:artifact BUILD-'), and replace '69' with the measured count.","Reword the premise so it makes no count claim, e.g. 'BUILD records whose markdown lacks the insrc:artifact marker cannot be opened through workflow.artifactContent', and cite an artifactContent read as the anchor for the mechanism.","If artifactContent turns out to fall back to the hash-named json when the marker is missing, drop the premise because the claimed failure does not occur."]}},{"claimId":"p23","ref":"c23","kind":"semantic","severity":"MED","premise":"ReviewReport at src/workflow/review/types.ts:146-156 has fields artifact, stage, verdict, findings, counts, template?, reviewedAt, model — and no revision or content-hash field.","evidence":"The evidence partly confirms the premise. `src/workflow/review/types.ts:144` opens `export interface ReviewReport {`. Line 146 is `readonly artifact: string;`, line 155 is `readonly reviewedAt: string;` and line 156 is `readonly model: string;`, so the range 146-156 covers the field body. The declaration itself is at :144, not :146. `src/workflow/review/template-review.ts:123` builds a complete report as `{ artifact: opts.artifact ?? stage, stage, verdict, findings, counts, template: template.id, reviewedAt, model }`. That matches the eight listed fields, and it compiles with no revision or hash field, so any such field cannot be required. However, lines 145 and 147-154 were not read, and the interface may continue past :156, so the full field list and the absence of an optional revision or hash field cannot be confirmed. One more signal: `src/workflow/__tests__/helpers/other-party-review.ts:74` puts a `reviewedBy` key next to `reviewedAt`/`model`. That suggests the report may carry an optional `reviewedBy` field that the premise leaves out. None of the matches shows a revision or content-hash key on any report literal.","action":"Change the anchor to `src/workflow/review/types.ts:144` (where the interface is declared). Read the full interface body to confirm the field list, and either add `reviewedBy?` if it is a ReviewReport field or note that it is not. The central claim, that there is no revision or content-hash field, is consistent with the evidence. The change it prescribes (adding a revision or freshness link) still applies either way.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor is off by two lines: the interface is declared at :144, and :146 is its first field. The enumerated field list cannot be fully confirmed from the evidence, and the test helper hints at a possible optional `reviewedBy` field. Neither problem changes what the premise prescribes, so this is MED. A person should confirm the field list before the wording changes.","artifactEdits":[{"find":"src/workflow/review/types.ts:146-156 (ReviewReport)","replace":"src/workflow/review/types.ts:144-156 (ReviewReport)"}],"options":["Re-anchor to types.ts:144-156 and keep the field list as is (if reading the file confirms `reviewedBy` is not on ReviewReport)","Re-anchor to types.ts:144 and add `reviewedBy?` to the listed fields (if reading the file confirms it is a ReviewReport field)","Keep the anchor and reword it to \"fields include artifact, stage, verdict, findings, counts, template?, reviewedAt, model\", so the list is not claimed to be complete"]}},{"claimId":"p24","ref":"c23","kind":"citation","severity":"MED","premise":"src/workflow/chain.ts:48-51 carries per-story stale / staleReason fields — the only recorded staleness signal.","evidence":"The anchor resolves correctly. The read of src/workflow/chain.ts:48 gives `readonly hasLld: boolean;` and chain.ts:51 gives `readonly staleReason?: string;`, so lines 48-51 are the per-story chain-mark fields, including stale and staleReason. The \"only recorded staleness signal\" part goes further than the evidence supports. src/workflow/amendments/staleness.ts:45 declares its own `readonly staleReason?: string;`, and staleness.ts:85/90/95 set `stale: true, staleReason: 'malformed'` from the stored and current effective hash. So the chain.ts fields are computed and passed along from staleness.ts; they are not stored on their own. The tests confirm the computed values: effective-and-staleness.test.ts:191/203 (`amendment-${AMD1}`, 'hld-rerun') and amendments-e2e.test.ts:339. docs/plans/workflow-design.md:538 says staleReason is \"set post-hoc when framework marks stale\", which also points to a computed value. This staleness compares an LLD to its HLD and says nothing about whether a review is stale. The premise's conclusion still holds: ReviewReport has no revision or content-hash field, and no review-staleness signal exists. The only problem is the claim about what \"only\" covers and where the value comes from.","action":"Keep the citation, but rewrite the claim. chain.ts:48-51 shows LLD-vs-HLD staleness that src/workflow/amendments/staleness.ts computes from hldEffectiveHash. Neither file records review staleness. Cite staleness.ts:45 as the source of the value, and drop \"the only recorded staleness signal\" or narrow it.","fixability":"assisted","proposedFix":{"rationale":"The anchor and fields check out. The overreach is the word 'only' and treating the value as stored on disk. staleness.ts:45 is the source of the value, and it only measures LLD-vs-HLD staleness. The DEF's conclusion that no review-revision signal exists is unaffected.","artifactEdits":[{"find":"src/workflow/chain.ts:48-51 (per-story stale / staleReason)","replace":"src/workflow/chain.ts:48-51 (per-story LLD-vs-HLD stale / staleReason, computed by src/workflow/amendments/staleness.ts:45 from hldEffectiveHash; no review-staleness signal)"}],"options":["Change the citation text to call it 'per-story LLD-vs-HLD stale / staleReason (computed in src/workflow/amendments/staleness.ts:45 from hldEffectiveHash; never applies to reviews)'.","Remove the chain.ts citation from c23 and rely only on ReviewReport (review/types.ts:146-156) having no